In Java, the `==` operator checks for reference equality, i.e., whether two objects refer to the same instance in memory. In contrast, the `.equals()` method is a class-specific method that checks for value equality, i.e., whether two objects have the same value or content.

For string comparison in Java, you should always use the `.equals()` method instead of the `==` operator. Here's why:

1. String comparison using `==` operator:

   ```java
   String str1 = "Hello";
   String str2 = "Hello";

   if (str1 == str2) {
       System.out.println("Both strings refer to the same object.");
   }
   ```

   This code will always print "Both strings refer to the same object." because both `str1` and `str2` refer to the same string literal in memory. However, if you create two different string objects with the same content, `==` will not work:

   ```java
   String str1 = new String("Hello");
   String str2 = new String("Hello");

   if (str1 == str2) {
       System.out.println("Both strings refer to the same object.");
   }
   ```

   This code will always print "Both strings do not refer to the same object." because `str1` and `str2` are two different objects, even though they have the same content.

2. String comparison using `.equals()` method:

   ```java
   String str1 = "Hello";
   String str2 = "Hello";

   if (str1.equals(str2)) {
       System.out.println("Both strings have the same value.");
   }
   ```

   This code will always print "Both strings have the same value." because the `.equals()` method checks for value equality, regardless of whether the objects refer to the same instance or not.

   ```java
   String str1 = new String("Hello");
   String str2 = new String("Hello");

   if (str1.equals(str2)) {
       System.out.println("Both strings have the same value.");
   }
   ```

   This code will also print "Both strings have the same value." because the `.equals()` method compares the actual content of the strings, not their references.

In summary, when comparing strings in Java, you should always use the `.equals()` method instead of the `==` operator. The `==` operator is only suitable for comparing references, not values. The `.equals()` method provides a safe and reliable way to check for value equality between string objects.

